          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            When discussing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language"} languages
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                finite automata
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/finite+automata"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %em alphabet
            for the language. The next step is to form the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monoid"} free monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^*
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^*
            thus corresponding to sequences of operations. Usually the system will not allow arbitrary sequences of operations to be performed, and so the system determines a language, namely the allowable execution sequences. Describing the language, helps describe and determine the overall ‘system’.
          %p
            If the system being studied has some concurrency in it, so that certain of the operations are (causally) independent of each other, then one way of modelling such a system is to use a
            = succeed "," do
              %em (Mazurkiewicz) trace alphabet
          .un-definition
            %h2#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %em (Mazurkiewicz) trace alphabet
              is a pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma, I)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a (usually finite) set of
              %em actions
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \subseteq \Sigma \times \Sigma
              is an
              %em irreflexive
              and
              %em symmetric
              relation.
          %p
            The relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is usually referred to as
            = succeed "." do
              %em independence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = (\Sigma \times \Sigma) - I
            is called the
            %em dependency
            relation. It will be reflexive and symmetric. (Such a sense of ‘dependency relation’ is interpreted in a related but slightly different way from that in the use of
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dependency_graph"} dependency graphs
            in project planning.)
